Chapter: IdentityOps
  cites Foreword chapter Identity

 current-user and authenticate are performable identity operations now,
 not unhandled effect operations that read a zeroed slot. This program
 exercises both. With no identity word set and the private key locked
 -- the state a plain test boot is in -- both are the empty string.
 After identity-set 0 42, current-user reflects the new word; the key
 is still locked, so authenticate stays empty. Each effectful set is
 threaded through a step helper in argument position, the sanctioned
 position for an effectful call outside an act-bind, exactly as
 identity-basic does. The boot grant derived from opening's own
 manifest supplies the Identity capability bit the builtins check.

Section: Body

  before-set : Integer -> [Identity] Text
  before-set (x) =
    if current-user /= "" then "FAIL: user before set"
    else if authenticate /= "" then "FAIL: auth before set"
    else "PASS-before"

  after-set : Integer -> [Identity] Text
  after-set (s0) =
    if s0 /= 0 then "FAIL: set returned error"
    else if current-user /= "id:42" then "FAIL: user after set"
    else if authenticate /= "" then "FAIL: auth still locked"
    else "PASS"

  run-ops : Integer -> [Identity] Text
  run-ops (x) =
    if before-set 0 /= "PASS-before" then "FAIL: before-set"
    else after-set (identity-set 0 42)

  opening : [Console, Identity] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (run-ops 0)
  end
